@*
    You.

    WHAT THE PHONE KNOWS ABOUT YOU - ALL OF IT. This screen showed five identity
    fields while the store underneath held work history, skills, education,
    certificates and languages too, so the completeness bar printed at the top
    could not pass 50% however carefully somebody filled it in: the fields on show
    are worth 9 of the 18 points the career store weighs. A person filling in every
    box they could see and being told they were half done is being told they failed
    at something they were never shown.

    AND IT WAS A FORM. The CV screen next door opens with the reason: "one
    question at a time, not a form - a form asks somebody with no CV to produce one
    before they can start." Then this screen, the one place the app talks about the
    person themselves, was five empty boxes and 0%. So the way in is to be ASKED,
    and the boxes are for correcting what the asking got.

    Folded, one section at a time, for the same reason as Services and Settings:
    six headings and their counts fit on a 344px screen, and a section below the
    fold is a section nobody checks.

    CLOSED AT REST, AND IT MAY SCROLL ONCE OPENED. It opened on the identity
    fields and clipped what would not fit, which lost "Forget me" off the bottom
    edge - a right somebody could not reach. Six headings fit; five text boxes on
    top of them do not, and that is a thing to scroll rather than a thing to cut.
*@
@page "/you"
@inject IProfile Store
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<div class="you">

    @*
        THE BAR EARNS ITS PLACE NOW, because everything it counts is on this
        screen. Beside it, the one absent thing worth most - computed where the
        weights live, so the sentence and the bar cannot disagree.
    *@
    <div class="bar-line">
        <span class="done">@($"{_profile.Completeness * 100:0}%")</span>
        <span class="track"><span style="width:@($"{_profile.Completeness * 100:0}%")"></span></span>
    </div>

    @if (_profile.Missing.Length > 0)
    {
        <p class="missing">@_profile.Missing</p>
    }

    <button class="fold @(_open == 0 ? "fold-on" : null)" @onclick="() => Open(0)"
            aria-expanded="@(_open == 0)">
        <span class="fold-title">About you</span>
        <span class="fold-now">@Named</span>
        <span class="fold-chevron" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
    </button>

    @if (_open == 0)
    {
        @foreach (var fact in _profile.Facts)
        {
            @*
                LABEL AND BOX ON ONE LINE. Stacked, five fields took the whole
                screen and pushed the other five sections off the bottom - which is
                how this screen came to look as though the identity fields were all
                there was.
            *@
            <label class="line" @key="fact.Key">
                <span class="line-label">@fact.Label</span>
                <input class="line-input" placeholder="@fact.Hint" value="@fact.Value"
                       @onchange="e => Save(fact.Key, e)" />
            </label>
        }
    }

    @for (var i = 0; i < _profile.Sections.Count; i++)
    {
        var index = i + 1;
        @* NOT `section`: @section is a Razor DIRECTIVE, so `@section.Title`
           parses as that directive and the file stops compiling. *@
        var part = _profile.Sections[i];

        <button class="fold @(_open == index ? "fold-on" : null)" @onclick="() => Open(index)"
                aria-expanded="@(_open == index)">
            <span class="fold-title">@part.Title</span>
            <span class="fold-now">@(part.Entries.Count == 0 ? "—" : part.Entries.Count.ToString())</span>
            <span class="fold-chevron" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        </button>

        @if (_open == index)
        {
            @if (part.Entries.Count == 0)
            {
                @*
                    WHAT WOULD GO HERE AND WHY, not "nothing yet". Somebody who
                    does not know a licence code decides applications outright has
                    no reason to go and add one.
                *@
                <p class="nothing">@part.Nothing</p>
                <button class="ask" @onclick="Interview">Tell it about this</button>
            }
            else
            {
                @foreach (var entry in part.Entries)
                {
                    @* Keyed on the section as well as the row id: ids come from
                       five different tables and only happen not to collide because
                       one section is open at a time. *@
                    var key = part.Key + entry.Id;

                    <div class="line" @key="key">
                        <span class="line-text">
                            <span class="line-title">@entry.Title</span>
                            @if (entry.Detail.Length > 0)
                            {
                                <span class="line-sub">@entry.Detail</span>
                            }
                        </span>
                        @*
                            REMOVE, NOT EDIT. A job with its dates and what was
                            achieved in it does not fit in a text box; what a person
                            needs here is to strike out what is wrong or stale, and
                            adding happens where it can be asked about properly.
                        *@
                        <button class="strike" @onclick="() => Remove(part, entry)"
                                aria-label="@($"Remove {entry.Title}")">Remove</button>
                    </div>
                }
            }
        }
    }

    <div class="actions">
        <button class="soft" @onclick="Interview">@(_profile.Completeness > 0 ? "Add to this" : "Tell it about you")</button>
        <button class="soft-2" @onclick='() => Nav.NavigateTo("career")'>Make my CV</button>
    </div>

    @*
        FORGET IS OUT OF THE ROW AND ASKS FIRST.

        It sat beside "Make my CV" as an equal, one tap from deleting a name, a
        phone number and an entire employment history with nothing in between. The
        right to erase everything is real - it is the other half of promising this
        never leaves the phone - and it is still one tap away. It just is not one
        tap from where somebody's thumb already is.
    *@
    @if (_confirming)
    {
        <div class="confirm">
            <span class="confirm-text">Delete everything it knows about you? This cannot be undone.</span>
            <button class="danger" @onclick="Forget">Delete it</button>
            <button class="strike" @onclick="() => _confirming = false">Keep it</button>
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <button class="quiet" @onclick="() => _confirming = true">Forget me</button>
    }

    @if (_said is not null)
    {
        <p class="said">@_said</p>
    }

</div>

@code {
    Profile _profile = new([], [], 0, "");

    /// <summary>Which section is open, or -1 for none.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// ALL CLOSED TO BEGIN WITH. It opened on the identity fields, and five text
    /// boxes plus six headings plus the buttons is more than a 344px phone has -
    /// so the screen arrived already overflowing, with "Forget me" cut off the
    /// bottom. Closed, the whole shape of what the app knows fits at once, which
    /// is what this screen is for; opening one is a tap somebody chose to make.
    /// </remarks>
    int _open = -1;

    bool _confirming;
    string? _said;

    /// <summary>The name, for the closed heading.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// So the commonest reason to open this screen - checking it still knows who
    /// you are - needs no taps at all. Same reasoning as the wake phrase on the
    /// settings tab.
    /// </remarks>
    string Named
    {
        get
        {
            var name = _profile.Facts.FirstOrDefault(f => f.Key == "name")?.Value;
            return string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(name) ? "—" : name;
        }
    }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() => _profile = await Store.LoadAsync();

    void Open(int index) => _open = _open == index ? -1 : index;

    /// <summary>
    /// Saved on leaving the field, not on every keystroke.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A write per character would hit SQLite on every letter of somebody's name.
    /// @onchange fires when the field loses focus, which is also when a person has
    /// finished the thought - and there is no save button, so what is on screen is
    /// always what is stored.
    /// </remarks>
    async Task Save(string key, ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        await Store.SetAsync(key, e.Value?.ToString() ?? "");
        _profile = await Store.LoadAsync();
    }

    async Task Remove(ProfileSection section, ProfileEntry entry)
    {
        await Store.RemoveAsync(section.Key, entry.Id);
        _profile = await Store.LoadAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The way things get ONTO this screen.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The CV interview is what asks the questions - where you worked, for how
    /// long, what you actually did there - and it writes into the same store this
    /// screen reads. Calling it "make my CV" hid that: somebody who wanted the
    /// phone to know them, and did not want a CV, had no door.
    /// </remarks>
    void Interview() => Nav.NavigateTo("career");

    async Task Forget()
    {
        await Store.ForgetAsync();
        _profile = await Store.LoadAsync();
        _confirming = false;
        _said = "Everything it knew about you is gone from this phone.";
    }
}
